WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Command-line arguments are often the first way a Bash script receives instructions.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:17.880
They may decide what file is processed, which environment is targeted, whether a script runs in preview or apply mode, what service is restarted, where an artifact is written, or which operation is performed.

3
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.940
That makes arguments more than convenience values.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.640
They are control inputs that can steer meaningful behavior.

5
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:34.420
An unvalidated request may select the wrong target, an unsupported destination, high-impact logic with an empty value, or a mode that was never intended.

6
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.300
A script that does not validate arguments can behave unpredictably.

7
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:48.080
It may process the wrong target, write to an unsupported destination, continue into high-impact logic with an empty value, or treat a value as a mode that was never intended.

8
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:56.660
In operational scripts, those mistakes can affect deployments, backups, service maintenance, data movement, or incident response workflows.

9
00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.420
There is an important difference between supplied input and valid input.

10
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.200
If a person, scheduler, pipeline, or another script provides a value, that only proves the value exists.

11
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.900
It does not prove the value is allowed, safe, complete, or meaningful for this workflow.

12
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.080
Defensive scripts define the accepted command line before action begins and reject unsupported input early.

13
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.000
Secure argument handling has two goals.

14
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:28.900
First, validate arguments before the script touches files, services, deployments, or sensitive data.

15
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.360
Second, preserve the meaning of validated values when passing them to functions or external commands.

16
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:42.460
Bash makes it easy to accidentally split, expand, or reinterpret values.

17
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:49.120
A safer script keeps command-line data intentional from the first argument check to the final command call.
